              # Concepts


              This repository follows Open Knowledge Format (OKF) v0.2. A
              concept is a UTF-8 `.md` file with YAML

              frontmatter followed by a portable Markdown body. Every concept
              must have parseable frontmatter and

              a non-empty `type`; `title` and `description` are recommended for
              human navigation, previews, and

              search.


              ```yaml

              ---

              type: Playbook

              title: Clear title for the page

              description: One-line summary of the concept.

              resource: https://example.com/canonical-resource

              tags: [sales, onboarding]

              generated:
                by: WORKFLOW_SIGNATURE_FROM_EXECUTION_CONTEXT
                at: CURRENT_DATETIME_FROM_EXECUTION_CONTEXT
              ---

              ```


              ### Required workflow provenance


              The workflow execution context contains a ready-to-copy
              `generated` mapping with the exact workflow

              signature and current datetime for this run. Whenever you create a
              concept or meaningfully update a

              concept's frontmatter or Markdown body, copy that mapping into the
              concept's leading YAML

              frontmatter. This requirement applies to both new and existing
              concepts.


              The result must have this nested shape, using the exact values
              supplied in the execution context

              rather than these placeholder names:


              ```yaml

              ---

              type: <non-empty concept type>

              generated:
                by: <exact workflow signature>
                at: <exact current datetime>
              ---

              ```


              Put `generated` inside the opening frontmatter block, not in the
              Markdown body, and do not write

              literal dotted keys named `generated.by` or `generated.at`.
              Replace the complete `generated` mapping

              when updating a concept, even when it already has generation
              provenance. Preserve an existing

              `generated` mapping only when the concept's content is left
              unchanged. Do not stamp source files

              that you only read or non-concept assets that you create.


              ### Concept properties


              Before choosing a `type`, list the values already used by concepts
              and inspect direct `types/*.md`

              registry entries. Reuse an existing type when its meaning fits. If
              none fits, choose a new short,

              descriptive type; unknown and repository-specific types are valid
              and do not require a registry

              entry. Use `resource` only for the canonical URI or bundle path of
              the asset the concept describes.

              Use `tags` as a YAML list of short strings. `generated` records
              who or what produced the current

              content; `sources` records materials it derives from. The optional
              `verified`, `status`, and

              `stale_after` fields describe trust and lifecycle. Every OKF
              timestamp is ISO 8601 with an explicit

              UTC offset.


              Producers may add domain-specific frontmatter. Preserve unknown
              fields and useful existing metadata

              when round-tripping a concept. Preserve `sources` and verification
              history unless the workflow has

              evidence to change them. Use the provided workflow signature and
              datetime for `generated`; do not

              invent other provenance, verification, actors, timestamps, or
              canonical resources.


              Before writing, search for relevant concepts and inspect exact
              files rather than guessing from

              filenames or snippets. Follow Markdown links and explicit `parent`
              relationships when they provide

              necessary context. Bundle-relative links begin with `/`; relative
              links resolve from the current

              document. Broken links and missing registry entries do not make a
              concept invalid.


              Use structural Markdown suited to the material. Do not force fixed
              body sections. Files named

              `index.md` and `log.md` are reserved and are not concepts. Do not
              create new `.markdown` files;

              preserve that suffix only when editing a legacy file.


              Before applying a patch that creates or updates a concept, verify
              that it begins with `---`, has a

              closing `---`, includes a non-empty `type`, includes the required
              `generated` mapping, and follows

              any applicable type schema described below.


              ## Optional type and tag registries


              The repository may document vocabulary as ordinary concepts:


              - `types/<type>.md` with `type: Type` documents the exact,
              case-sensitive `type` value named by the
                filename.
              - `tags/<tag>.md` with `type: Tag` documents the exact,
              case-sensitive tag value named by the
                filename.

              Definitions are optional: a type or tag remains valid when its
              registry concept does not exist.

              Inspect matching registry concepts when available and reuse
              documented vocabulary when it fits. Do

              not automatically create a definition for every value; create or
              update registry concepts only when

              the workflow is explicitly organizing or documenting the
              repository vocabulary.


              A Type concept may contain a JSON Schema 2020-12 definition in its
              `schema` frontmatter property.

              Before creating or changing domain-specific properties on a
              concept, read its exact

              `types/<type>.md` definition when present and follow that schema.
              The schema applies only to

              domain-specific properties, not standard OKF fields or okc's
              `parent` and `schema` fields. Preserve

              existing properties that the schema does not render unless the
              task explicitly removes them.


              ### Defining a type


              When the task calls for a new type definition, create a direct
              child of `types/` whose filename is

              the exact type value, such as `types/Customer.md`. The definition
              is itself a concept of type

              `Type`:


              ```yaml

              ---

              type: Type

              title: Customer

              description: An organization that uses our product.

              schema:
                type: object
                properties:
                  accountOwner:
                    type: string
                required: [accountOwner]
              ---

              ```


              Add the execution context's `generated` mapping and explain the
              type's meaning in the body.

              Instances use `type: Customer` and the domain properties described
              by this definition.


              ### Standard properties


              - `type`: the non-empty, case-sensitive concept type. `title` and
              `description` describe the page.

              - `resource`: the canonical URI or bundle path of the asset
              described, not a list of citations.

              - `tags`: a list of short strings; optional definitions live in
              `tags/<tag>.md` with `type: Tag`.

              - `sources`: a list of mappings with `resource` and optional `id`,
              `title`, `author`, `usage_count`,
                `last_modified`, and `usage_window`. Use a source's `id` for body footnotes when useful.
              - `usage_window`: a mapping with `from` and `to` timestamps
              framing source usage counts. A source
                may
                override it with its own window. Do not invent usage statistics.
              - `generated`: a mapping with `by` and `at` for the producer and
              time of the current content.

              - `verified`: one mapping or a list of mappings, each with `by`
              and `at`, recording independent
                verification. Generation does not count as verification; preserve existing verification history.
              - `status`: `draft`, `stable`, or `deprecated`; omission means
              stable. `stale_after` is the
                timestamp
                on or after which a concept is stale.
              - Attested Computation fields: `runtime` is required for `type:
              Attested Computation`; `parameters`
                lists mappings with `name`, `type`, and `required`; `computation` points to code; `executor` has a
                `resource` and optional `receipt` field names; `attester` has a `resource` for attestation code.
              - okc extensions: `parent` explicitly identifies a facet's parent;
              `schema` on a Type definition
                describes domain properties; `icon` is an icon name or an object with `name` and optional `color`.

              All timestamps use ISO 8601 with an explicit UTC offset. Actor
              identifiers are producer-defined;

              preserve unfamiliar existing identifiers instead of rewriting
              them.


              ## Concept facets


              A facet is a separate markdown concept that presents a distinct
              aspect of a parent concept. Create a

              facet when the workflow explicitly needs independently addressable
              content or policy metadata; do

              not split an ordinary document into facets only because it has
              multiple sections.


              Store facets in a sibling directory named after the parent file
              without its extension. For example,

              facets of `customers/acme.md` belong under `customers/acme/`:


              ```text

              customers/acme.md

              customers/acme/financials.md

              customers/acme/financials/forecast.md

              ```


              Every facet must declare its direct parent with the `parent`
              frontmatter property. Prefer a

              bundle-relative path that starts with `/` and includes the `.md`
              extension:


              ```yaml

              ---

              type: Financials

              title: Acme financials

              parent: /customers/acme.md

              policy: finance-team

              ---

              ```


              - A nested facet points to its immediate facet parent, such as
              `parent:
                /customers/acme/financials.md`.
              - The directory layout is an authoring convention; the explicit
              `parent` property defines the
                relationship.
              - Preserve `parent` and `policy` when editing a facet unless the
              workflow intentionally changes
                them.
              - When moving or renaming a parent, update known facet references
              in the same change when
                permissions allow.
              - `policy` is currently metadata for a future authorization
              system. Do not claim that writing it
                enforces access or changes the workflow's actual file permissions.
